WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.347 --> 00:00:14.549
as a baby and left to die, Oedipus survived to rule as a proud king.

2
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:19.631
But he was a man marked by fate, and the tragedy of his birth followed him his whole

3
00:00:19.671 --> 00:00:26.514
life. At the birth of their son, King Laius and Queen Jocasta of Thebes received an ominous prophecy

4
00:00:26.574 --> 00:00:28.715
from the mysterious oracle at Delphi.

5
00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:31.736
Their son would kill his father and marry his mother.

6
00:00:32.652 --> 00:00:37.854
Anxious to avoid this fate, Laius gave his newborn son to a shepherd with orders to leave the

7
00:00:37.914 --> 00:00:40.935
infant on the slopes of Mount Cithaeron to die.

8
00:00:41.815 --> 00:00:46.877
Rather than abandon the child as instructed, the shepherd took pity on him and gave him to another

9
00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:49.737
shepherd, who took the baby to the city of Corinth.

10
00:00:50.678 --> 00:00:56.940
The childless rulers of Corinth, King Polybus and Queen Merope, decided to raise him as their own, naming

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:57.760
him Oedipus.

12
00:00:58.952 --> 00:01:03.655
Oedipus grew to be a strong young man, content to live as a prince in the Corinthian court.

13
00:01:04.715 --> 00:01:10.498
One day, he overheard a local shepherd say that Prince Oedipus was not the true son of Polybus

14
00:01:10.678 --> 00:01:11.299
and Merope.

15
00:01:12.599 --> 00:01:18.503
When Oedipus confronted his parents, the king and queen denied the accusation, but he decided to visit the

16
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:20.964
oracle of Delphi to get a definitive answer.

17
00:01:21.884 --> 00:01:26.827
Inside the darkened temple, an old priestess sat on a stool over an ancient hole in the earth.

18
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:30.549
Reverently, Oedipus approached and asked his question.

19
00:01:31.309 --> 00:01:35.790
Vapors rose from the opening, clouding the old woman's face as she fell into a trance.

20
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.331
Then she spoke the oracle's words.

21
00:01:39.271 --> 00:01:46.013
Instead of telling Oedipus who his real parents were, however, the oracle responded with a terrifying prophecy: Oedipus

22
00:01:46.073 --> 00:01:49.014
was destined to murder his father and take his mother as his wife.

23
00:01:49.894 --> 00:01:52.215
The prince was shocked by the horrible prediction.

24
00:01:52.955 --> 00:01:56.136
There was only one way to avoid this terrible destiny, he decided.

25
00:01:56.704 --> 00:01:58.565
He must never return home to Corinth.

26
00:01:59.325 --> 00:02:02.726
From Delphi, Oedipus set out across the mountains to Thebes.

27
00:02:03.586 --> 00:02:09.087
In a narrow mountain pass, Oedipus met an older man hurriedly driving a chariot toward Delphi.

28
00:02:10.008 --> 00:02:15.089
Neither Oedipus nor the man was willing to cede the right-of-way, and their argument soon turned violent.

29
00:02:15.809 --> 00:02:20.471
The older man struck at Oedipus, and Oedipus, in turn, knocked him out of his chariot and into

30
00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:20.991
the road.

31
00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:25.672
Immediately, the old man was trampled by his horses and died.

32
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:32.206
The man's attendants fell upon Oedipus, and he fought and killed them all except one—a young man who

33
00:02:32.266 --> 00:02:33.206
ran away in fear.

34
00:02:33.226 --> 00:02:35.589
Oedipus continued on his journey.

35
00:02:36.329 --> 00:02:38.872
At the gates of Thebes, a new challenge awaited.

36
00:02:39.572 --> 00:02:44.197
The city had long been troubled by the sphinx, a terrible monster with the body of a winged

37
00:02:44.257 --> 00:02:45.578
lion and the head of a woman.

38
00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:52.496
Lying in wait at the city gates, the sphinx demanded that all travelers answer the following riddle: "What

39
00:02:52.536 --> 00:02:57.180
goes on four feet in the morning, on two in the afternoon, and on three in the evening?"

40
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:03.104
When the hapless travelers could not answer the cryptic question, the sphinx strangled and devoured them.

41
00:03:03.944 --> 00:03:06.326
The sphinx confronted Oedipus with her enigma.

42
00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:10.389
Determined to outwit her, he retreated to a safe place to think.

43
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:14.692
After days of contemplation, he returned to the gates with his answer.

44
00:03:15.453 --> 00:03:19.936
When the sphinx posed her question, Oedipus spoke: "Man is the answer.

45
00:03:20.712 --> 00:03:24.636
It is man that goes on all fours in the early part of his life, then on two

46
00:03:24.716 --> 00:03:28.439
feet in his maturity, and finally leans on a staff in old age."

47
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.185
Her riddle solved, the sphinx threw herself off a nearby cliff and perished on the rocks below.

48
00:03:35.106 --> 00:03:38.709
When Oedipus entered the city, news of his victory preceded him.

49
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.232
The city rejoiced and welcomed the young prince as a hero.

50
00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:45.836
A crowd led him to the house of the queen's brother, Creon.

51
00:03:46.716 --> 00:03:51.777
There, Oedipus learned that King Laius had been killed on the road to the Delphic oracle when he

52
00:03:51.817 --> 00:03:54.518
had gone to ask how to free his city from the sphinx.

53
00:03:55.458 --> 00:04:00.160
The citizens of Thebes, Creon explained, would be happy if Oedipus became their new king.

54
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.081
Oedipus accepted and was married to the widow Jocasta.

55
00:04:04.921 --> 00:04:10.502
In the years that followed, Oedipus ruled Thebes in comfort, and Jocasta bore him four children.

56
00:04:11.303 --> 00:04:13.783
But one summer, plagues beset the city.

57
00:04:14.563 --> 00:04:18.626
New deaths were reported every day, and wails of grief sounded in the streets.

58
00:04:19.487 --> 00:04:25.251
Creon undertook the journey to consult the Delphic oracle, and on his return, he revealed the oracle's message.

59
00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:31.377
The man who had murdered King Laius was living in Thebes unpunished, and the city was tainted by

60
00:04:31.417 --> 00:04:32.277
this man's sin.

61
00:04:33.298 --> 00:04:36.781
The people of Thebes had to bring King Laius' murderer to justice.

62
00:04:37.762 --> 00:04:43.987
On the steps of the palace, with Queen Jocasta at his side, Oedipus pronounced a dreadful curse upon

63
00:04:44.007 --> 00:04:47.490
the person who had caused so much misery and vowed to find him.

64
00:04:48.571 --> 00:04:54.115
He sent soldiers to scour the city and questioned everyone they brought before him, but none of them

65
00:04:54.195 --> 00:04:54.995
was the murderer.

66
00:04:55.896 --> 00:05:01.320
At last, Oedipus sent for the blind seer Tiresias and asked him to reveal the man's identity.

67
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.324
Tiresias answered that he knew who the criminal was, but that the truth would only cause more suffering.

68
00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:12.488
Oedipus demanded an answer, but Tiresias, trembling before him, refused.

69
00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.581
In a rage, Oedipus ordered the old seer dragged from the palace.

70
00:05:17.641 --> 00:05:23.923
In desperation, Oedipus called for one of Laius' former servants, the only witness to survive the attack on

71
00:05:23.963 --> 00:05:25.323
the king so many years ago.

72
00:05:25.343 --> 00:05:28.624
Oedipus listened intently to the servant's story.

73
00:05:29.104 --> 00:05:34.686
King Laius had encountered a young man along a narrow mountain pass, and the two had engaged in

74
00:05:34.726 --> 00:05:35.466
a bloody battle.

75
00:05:36.226 --> 00:05:39.927
The old man had been knocked from his chariot and trampled by his horses.

76
00:05:40.888 --> 00:05:45.871
Suddenly, Oedipus realized the awful truth: he was the man who had killed the king!

77
00:05:46.431 --> 00:05:47.732
He had cursed himself!

78
00:05:48.593 --> 00:05:54.157
Oedipus dismissed the servant as an old shepherd approached; he had been sent with news from Corinth that

79
00:05:54.217 --> 00:05:55.478
King Polybus was dead.

80
00:05:56.358 --> 00:05:57.819
Relief washed over Oedipus.

81
00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:05.104
He may have been responsible for King Laius' death, but the oracle's terrible prophecy had been defeated: Oedipus

82
00:06:05.144 --> 00:06:06.485
had not killed his father.

83
00:06:07.386 --> 00:06:10.488
The shepherd turned to Oedipus and gently put his hand on his shoulder.

84
00:06:11.348 --> 00:06:14.150
"You are not the child of Polybus," he explained.

85
00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:15.971
"You are the son of Laius.

86
00:06:16.772 --> 00:06:21.275
You were rescued from a terrible fate as a baby, and I brought you to the Corinthian court."

87
00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:28.900
The oracle's prediction had come true after all: Oedipus realized that he had unwittingly murdered his father and

88
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.000
married his own mother.

89
00:06:30.981 --> 00:06:33.603
It was his guilt that was polluting the city of Thebes.

90
00:06:34.624 --> 00:06:40.788
With a heavy heart, Oedipus went to Jocasta's room to share the hateful news, but she had already

91
00:06:40.828 --> 00:06:44.450
heard. Unable to bear the knowledge, she had hanged herself.

92
00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:50.395
Grief-stricken, Oedipus took two brooches from the queen's dress and gouged out his own eyes.

93
00:06:51.256 --> 00:06:56.380
Oedipus knew he must leave Thebes, and in the care of his eldest daughter, he left the palace.

94
00:06:57.241 --> 00:07:02.285
Cursed by fate and now as blind as the old seer Tiresias, he passed through the same city

95
00:07:02.325 --> 00:07:06.008
gates he had so triumphantly entered in his youth, never to return.
